  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nPresent trends with models continue to see the upper-level trough\noff the West Coast continue its eastward trek as it ingest \nwhatever remains of Tropical Storm Rachel. For Monday, this \ncontinued trek still remains a little too far west of the \nPanhandles, to have much impact outside of still well maintained \nsouthwesterly upper-level flow and dry weather. However, as we \nmove into the overnight and Tuesday, most models agree to the \ntrough forming a closed off upper-level low across Southern \nNevada. This low as well as well as an upper-level high to our \nsoutheast will work in tandem to once again force moisture out of \nboth gulfs and into the Southern Great Plains through what remains\nof next week. Added to this, is the potential for what remains of\nthe now named Hurricane Simon to also get ingested into this low \nas it pushes through. Overall this very familiar combination does \nbring potential for a very active middle of next week.\n\nWhile this set may sound familiar given its similarity to our last\nbig rain event, there are still some very distinct uncertainties\nthat could damper our chances. For starters, present model\nindications does not see the high nearly as strong as the last\ntime, which will make some of this moisture transport a lot slower\nand weaker. This means amounts will struggle to be as widespread\nacross the area when the systems fully moves across and starts\nlifting and producing storms. In association to that, present\nmodels have also seen the closed low taking a much more northerly\ntrack than last time, which does weaken our lift potential as well\nas introduce some increase chances of drier air also being mixed\nin this go around. Should said trends come to pass, then it is\nlikely that impacts will be much more localized than last time\nwith only a few areas of the Panhandles truly seeing that heavy to\ntorrential rainfall. As it stands present NBM is siding with this\nmore localized look with chances more focused across the Eastern\nPanhandles at around 30 to 60 percent with a 15 percent chance to\nsee strong to severe thunderstorms Tuesday. Still, present soil \nmoisture is running very high with present reports this weekend \nseeing the Western Panhandles in the 90 to 100 percentile range. \nThis would mean that those area would not need to see much \nprecipitation for flooding to occur. Regardless, all models and \nemblems agree that the set up and eventual passage of this system \nwill leave the Panhandles cooler as afternoon highs fall into the \n70s starting Wednesday with with the potential to even see highs \nin the 60s by next Saturday. &&",
